<urn:usf:pattern-neutrality-rule:1> <urn:usf:predicate:invariant> "No text of a Future Use Pattern may name a product, a user-interface framework, a markup or styling language, a repository path, or a file format the pattern does not itself concern. A pattern that names its first consumer is not reusable by a second." <urn:usf:graph:future-use-patterns> .
<urn:usf:pattern-neutrality-rule:1> <urn:usf:predicate:neutralityReason> "Reuse has to be a checkable property. A catalogue whose patterns quietly assume one product's nouns, one framework's components or one repository's layout is a catalogue with exactly one possible consumer, and nothing about its shape would reveal that." <urn:usf:graph:future-use-patterns> .
<urn:usf:pattern-neutrality-rule:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "The forbidden set is deliberately blunt and case-insensitive, and it will occasionally refuse an innocent phrasing. A pattern rewritten to avoid naming a format or a framework is nearly always the clearer pattern, so the false positives are cheap and the false negatives would not be." <urn:usf:graph:future-use-patterns> .
<urn:usf:pattern-neutrality-rule:1> <urn:usf:predicate:type> <urn:usf:type:PatternNeutralityRule> <urn:usf:graph:future-use-patterns> .
